    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d961">

                <pb facs="1016" n="1004" sameAs="frus:frus1941v01/pg1004"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.404/463: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Dickerson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kuibyshev</hi> (<hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Moscow</hi>)</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-12-24T13:00:00+03:00">December 24, 1941—1 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received December 25—10:03 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2098. Embassy’s 1765, October 6, noon. With reference to the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Moscow News Bulletin</hi><anchor n="39" xml:id="d961-f1"
                    /> article<anchor n="40" xml:id="d961-f2"/> on the support being given to the
                    Soviet war effort by the Russian Orthodox Church, excerpts from which are quoted
                    in the Embassy’s 2099<anchor n="41" xml:id="d961-f3"/> which will follow this,
                    the Embassy had seen no similar articles in the vernacular press and it is
                    believed that the interview as published represents no more than that lip
                    service to religious freedom to which Ambassador Harriman referred in the
                    Embassy’s telegram No. 1758, October 4, noon. Evidence in illustration of this
                    belief is afforded by the fact that it is known to <pb facs="1017" n="1005"
                        xml:id="pg1005"/> the Embassy that the Sofia Cathedral,<anchor n="42"
                        xml:id="d961-f4"/> the desecration of which by the Germans is represented as
                    having so aroused the indignation of Archbishop Andrey’s<anchor n="43"
                        xml:id="d961-f5"/> followers, was being used as a secular museum some years
                    ago. As the Department is aware the <hi rendition="#italic">Moscow News</hi> is
                    published almost exclusively for foreign consumption and from the reference to
                    the American Relief Administration<anchor n="44" xml:id="d961-f6"/> in the
                    article in question, it may reasonably be inferred that it was designed in
                    particular for American readers.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dickerson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d961-f1" n="39" xml:id="d961-f1-content">
                <p>Apparently a wartime successor to the <hi rendition="#italic">Moscow
                    News</hi>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d961-f2" n="40" xml:id="d961-f2-content">
                <p>The article was entitled: “Churchmen Back Country’s War Effort.”</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d961-f3" n="41" xml:id="d961-f3-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d961-f4" n="42" xml:id="d961-f4-content">
                <p>A famous ancient church in old Novgorod.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d961-f5" n="43" xml:id="d961-f5-content">
                <p>Archbishop Andrey of the Samara (Kuibyshev) bishopric of the Russian Orthodox
                    Church, of the conservative wing, gave the interview.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d961-f6" n="44" xml:id="d961-f6-content">
                <p>Archbishop Andrey had assisted staff members of the American Relief
                    Administration in 1921. Concerning the humanitarian activities of this
                    organization during the famine in the Soviet Union at that time, see footnote
                    32, <ref target="#pg730">p. 730</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
